\begin{abstract}
The Bullet Cluster (1E\,0657--56) constrains both gravitational dynamics and the nature of unseen mass through the separation of galaxies, X-ray plasma, and lensing convergence peaks. A timing argument requires the subclusters to attain their observed separation and encounter velocity within a Hubble time, \(t_{\rm orb}\lesssim H_0^{-1}\), thereby imposing a lower bound on the gravitating mass; however, the X-ray shock speed, \(v_{\rm sh}\sim 4500~{\rm km\,s^{-1}}\), need not equal the smaller halo--halo velocity inferred from hydrodynamic simulations. Ram-pressure stripping, \(P_{\rm ram}\simeq \rho_{\rm gas}v^2\), displaces the collisional baryonic plasma while galaxies and the dominant lensing mass remain approximately collisionless. Cold-dark-matter halos can generate compact mass peaks with cluster-scale masses of order \(10^{14}\!-\!10^{15}\,M_\odot\), whereas hot dark matter is limited by free streaming and phase-space bounds and generally requires neutrino masses near the eV scale to cluster appreciably. In the MOND lensing analysis of Angus et al.\ (2007), modified gravity alone did not reproduce the observed convergence map: an additional collisionless component, modeled with \(\sim2\,{\rm eV}\) neutrinos, was required around both subclusters. Thus the Bullet Cluster is not by itself a precision measurement of the dark-matter particle mass, but it strongly favors a substantial, weakly collisional gravitating component and sharply restricts purely baryonic or gravity-only explanations.
\end{abstract}
